    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-114280",
      "knowledge_point_id": "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e",
      "knowledge_point": "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/4bb767047996aad50a7991915c91774e0c19d9e5c1a7c60d66dd7a6afef0fa03.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?",
      "worked_solution": "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$   the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only.",
