      "knowledge_point_id": "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation",
      "problem": "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$",
      "worked_solution": "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-2x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1$  and $x=1.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =\\frac{2(1-(-2)^{2})}{(1+(-2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2(1-(0)^{2})}{(1+(0)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2(1-(2)^{2})}{(1+(2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are indeed points of inflection.",
